WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.450
This slide intentionally shows a grouping error
because PostgreSQL is protecting us

00:00:04.475 --> 00:00:10.112
from an ambiguous result The query asks for
diagnosis subject_id and

00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:14.938
COUNT star but it groups only by diagnosis That
creates a problem

00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:21.175
If one diagnosis group contains several different
subject IDs which single subject_id

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.638
should PostgreSQL display for the whole group There
is no correct answer

00:00:25.662 --> 00:00:30.450
unless we define one That is why PostgreSQL returns
an error instead

00:00:30.462 --> 00:00:34.588
of choosing an arbitrary value The general rule is
that a selected

00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:38.450
expression should either define the group be derived
consistently from the grouping

00:00:38.462 --> 00:00:43.238
columns or be calculated with an aggregate function
In a beginner query

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:48.412
the simplest rule is selected non-aggregate columns
belong in GROUP BY Run

00:00:48.438 --> 00:00:53.275
the example and read the error message Error
messages are useful teaching

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:58.162
tools They often tell you exactly which column is
causing the ambiguity

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.288
There are two possible ways to fix this query
depending on the

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.950
intended grain If we want one row per diagnosis
remove subject_id

00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:12.375
from SELECT If we want one row per patient and
diagnosis add

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.412
subject_id to GROUP BY The right fix comes from the
question not

00:01:17.438 --> 00:01:18.575
from syntax alone
